ath-academy-id "q-49003" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/03b4d6fea89eb695e0919aed4a82b1d25ff5e296338381192b5236f5295bef12.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by noticing that at $x=b$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f33639f6a54a2e0f35c52b684356ed832047ee538b8651e9ca9f75a542d366a9.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-49003/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49003"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-51432/selection/f26c6f4c71c2ff0fcabde0ae4367c6b6c2eb652284f3a43dd23c55d62197f5a9" :answer/id #uuid "27bedf57-8c65-5cfb-a3b5-0b59d2fc61e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-51432/selection/0b9c149634edf7a0be6bc3fd7d0ba84a46ec8ce1360d7eea39a20ac1da966651" :answer/id #uuid "3059c31a-419b-577d-9e2a-c9dc33926436" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ only" :db/en