e5a12b2ec9b154b0b59.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/c7a00e3b25a9fc1a9a589234d0beb421330b58cfe69d3e745f432c3f9be09485.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=d$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-142065/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-142065"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
